  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 128 PM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nLarge scale ridging continues to dominate the upper air pattern\nover the CONUS. The greatest chances for precipitation through\nthis weekend will be today where a moist airmass intersects\nweak synoptic forcing. Severe storms and flash flooding are\npossible with these storms through this afternoon and evening.\n\nAs we progress into the weekend, 500mb flow increases in the\nPacific Northwest, which will allow for the ridge axis to become\nmuch more angled and shift the axis eastward. Height rises and\nthermal ridging will allow temperatures to rise back into the\n90s by Sunday, continuing into Monday. More southwesterly flow\nleads to higher shear potential on Monday and Tuesday, however\nmoisture flux is very limited in the lowest levels. As a result,\ninstability is fairly weak and the signal for organized severe\nconvection is limited. As a front comes through the area\nTuesday, cooler temperatures prevail through the end of the week\nwith highs in the 80s.\n\n...SEVERE STORMS TODAY...\n\nScattered showers and thunderstorms will continue through the\nafternoon and evening hours, with likely expansion in some\nareas. Precipitable water is fairly high, between 1.5-1.9\", and\nflow is extremely weak aloft. Shear within the lowest 6km is at\nbest 5-15 knots and the bulk of shear is above 300mb. DCAPE is\nfairly high and above 1000 J/kg, especially the further south\nand east you go. As a result, downburst winds are likely at\ntimes this afternoon, but should be a fairly sporadic nature and\nunlikely to be widespread. With the very slow forward\npropagation of convection however, flash flooding may develop\nthis afternoon/evening if storms stay over one area long enough.\nWith the antecedent dry conditions though, it will likely be\ndifficult to get much more than urban flooding. While not\nimpossible to see rural flash flooding, the ongoing drought\nmakes the probability low. Storms will continue through the\nevening, but with near surface stabilization as time goes on,\ndownburst winds will likely become less widespread (or as\nwidespread as now).\n\nFinally, the sad truth with this is the scattered convection\nwill make some get decent rain amounts, but not everyone. Don't\nexpect a widespread heavy rainfall event. Some will get quite a\nbit but others may not see much.",
